### Recursive stop and hierarchical finalization (implemented)

Stopping an actor stops its whole subtree, and `Done` of an actor means its subtree is finished.

- **What happens.** Accepting the stop of an actor closes its spawn gate and returns. After the in-flight call and
  the start step, the actor asks every child it has committed to stop (outside every lock, each child through its own
  activation) and gives up its activation. It then waits for its **outstanding set**: the incarnation IDs of its
  committed children that have not signalled completion (a child still running its factory is committed, so it is
  counted), plus a reservation only between the reserve and the commit of one `Spawn` call. When
  the set is empty and the gate is closed, its finalization is scheduled once through the executor: `PostStop`, then
  the pending messages are dead-lettered (once per mailbox, FIFO), the name is released, the dead letters are
  published, the watchers are told, `Done` closes, and finally the actor signals its owner.
- **Guarantees.** Every descendant finalizes before its ancestor's `PostStop`; `Done(parent)` closed implies `Done`
  closed for every descendant; `PostStop` never overlaps an in-flight `Receive`; the name and the entry in the
  parent's `Child`/`Children` are gone before the watchers are told and before `Done` closes (goPekko's own contract,
  ADR D2); a child signals its owner only after `close(done)`. The order among siblings is unspecified.
- **Completion contract.** A child leaves the owner's outstanding set only through its own post-`Done` signal, by
  membership of its incarnation ID: a duplicate, stale (a respawned name), unknown or foreign signal changes nothing.
  Releasing the name does not count as completion. An owner with nothing outstanding (a leaf, or one whose children
  all finished earlier) finalizes at once, without waiting for any signal. A child committed while the owner is
  stopping is stopped by the owner's termination and awaited, whether its factory works or fails. Top-level actors signal `/user`, whose set returns to baseline; stopping `/user` stops them first, then `/system` and the root
  guardian follow (units U5a to U5c; `Terminate`, `WhenTerminated` and `Shutdown` are in "Termination of the System").
- **Stopping from inside.** `ctx.Stop` on a child, a sibling or the parent works as for any handle. A child that stops
  its parent finishes first, then the parent finalizes exactly once. Stopping a sibling only affects that sibling's
  subtree.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["stop accepted: gate closed"] --> B["in-flight call, start step"]
    B --> C["request the stop of every committed child"]
    C --> D{"children outstanding?"}
    D -->|none| F["finalization scheduled once"]
    D -->|some| W["wait"]
    W -->|"child signal after its Done"| D
    F --> G["PostStop, dead letters, name release, watchers, close Done"]
    G --> H["signal the owner"]
```

Limitations, stated without hiding them:

- A **factory that never returns** keeps its child's start and completion pending and blocks the owner's finalization,
  and so every ancestor's `Done`, forever; with an inline executor it also blocks `Spawn`, with the default executor
  it does not. A **handler that never returns** (a `Receive`, `PreStart`, or `PostStop` blocked on a
  channel) blocks the completion of its actor in the same way. Neither is solved; `Done` does not pretend otherwise.
- `byID` holds the live actors and the three guardians. An entry is retired when the owner counts the completion of the
  actor, after `Done` closed and the watchers were told (ADR 0024, decisions D1-D5; the unit is tracked in #541).
  Repeated create and stop returns the name tables, the outstanding sets and `byID` to baseline. A `Ref`, and the handles
  from `Child`, `Children` and `Parent`, keep their result after the retirement: `Tell` is a dead letter, `Ask` is
  `ErrRecipientTerminated`, `Done` is the same closed channel, `Watch` delivers `Terminated`. `ChildFailure.Child` carries
  that state only when the child was still registered when its failure was decided; for a child the registry had already
  retired it is a bare identity handle. A handle that carries only an ID (a user-implemented `Handle`, or that bare
  handle) gets a closed channel and `ErrUnknownHandle` from `Done` once the actor is retired. Retirement ends the System's reference to the actor; a `Ref` the application keeps still holds the
  cell. T6 is integrated: the release (#739, squash `a807fed2`, after #738) and its additional tests and evidence (#743, squash `d2a85040`). Issue #563 is closed with an evidence comment; #461 stays open for the consolidated actor closure. Evidence is in `migration/stage-1/cell-release-563/EXECUTION-RECORD.md` (sections 16 to 18): the historical author-run campaign detected 22 of 24 directed mutants, and M5 and M11 were later detected separately by one test (T04b), with no new full campaign and no total claimed; an independent review of that test (PASS_WITH_MINORS, at `177ed4c`); final gates run on `23771e9`, whose Go content is identical to the integrated head. The earlier independent read-only reviews found no blocker but ran no tests. The change releases the user instance, the factory and the failure and restart state of a finalized cell (`releaseTerminated`, after the watchers are told and before `Done` closes; that the call is adjacent to `close(c.done)` rests on inspection; T04b pins that the release runs under `c.mu` before `Done` closes, polling with a 1 ms cadence and a 10 s bound that only limits a failure); identity, the hierarchy node, `ctx`, `self`, `owner` and the counters stay, so a held `Ref` still pins that shell and, through `owner`, the parent's node. One heap measurement exists, for one scenario (200 actors stopped, every `Ref` held): reachable heap after GC about 0.4 to 0.8 MiB with the release against about 200 MiB without it. It measures reachability after GC, not memory returned to the operating system, and says nothing general. `-race` on Linux was not run, and the GitHub Actions jobs of #743 did not start because of billing, so there is no CI result. No Pekko parity is claimed.
