### Supervision logs (U9, #451)

`Config.Logger` (a `github.com/pablogore/kit-logger/pkg/logger.Logger`) receives one entry for each supervision decision that is `Resume`, `Restart` or `Stop`, written **before** the decision is applied (Pekko's `logFailure`): `Resume` at Warn, `Restart` and `Stop` at Error; `Escalate` (and a missing or failed decision) writes nothing. The message is the text of the cause; for an `*InitializationError` it is the text of the underlying error. The attributes are `child` (the failing child's ID), `directive` (`resume`, `restart`, `stop`) and, at Error only, `error` (the cause). Under `AllForOne` there is one entry per decision, not one per child. When a failure reaches the root guardian (for example `/user` after a `GuardianStrategy` escalates), the root writes first one Error entry with the message `guardian failed, shutting down system` (DTH-07, `ActorRefProvider.scala:518-522`) and the same attributes (`child` the failing guardian, `directive` `stop`, `error` the cause), then the generic entry of the `Stop`, then applies the `Stop` once. The specific entry is added to the generic one, not instead of it; each emission is protected against a panic or `runtime.Goexit` of the Logger. A nil Logger and a user-requested shutdown write nothing. Not implemented: the second site of Pekko (`ActorRefProvider.scala:479`, the bubble-walker of the root-of-roots), which has no counterpart here. Test: [`guardian_failed_log_internal_test.go`](actor/guardian_failed_log_internal_test.go).

`Strategy.WithLogging(false)` and `GuardianStrategy.WithLogging(false)` turn the entries off (Pekko's `loggingEnabled = false`). The flag is independent of the kind: on the zero `Strategy` (which means `DefaultStrategy`), on `DefaultStrategy()` and on a zero guardian strategy it silences the effective default; an explicit constructor and `WithLogging(true)` log. The log never changes a decision, the budget, a suspension count, the activation or an event; the order is snapshot (AllForOne), decider, log, apply, and `cause.Error()` is evaluated only for a logged decision, after the decider.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  F[child fails] --> D[decider]
  D -->|Escalate or no decision| A[apply]
  D -->|Resume, Restart, Stop| L{Logger set and logging on?}
  L -->|yes| W[write entry, protected] --> A
  L -->|no| A
```

